WEBVTT

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:03.120
Privacy and data protection are operational requirements,

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:07.535
not afterthoughts. Multi-jurisdiction intelligence work can involve personal data,

00:00:07.535 --> 00:00:12.030
sensitive business data, partner information, telemetry, fraud records, incident notes,

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:13.275
or regulated records.

00:00:13.275 --> 00:00:17.070
The team has to protect that material while still producing useful decision support.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:21.200
Data minimization is the discipline of collecting and sharing only what is necessary for

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:22.440
the authorized purpose.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:26.400
It starts before collection begins and continues through analysis, dissemination,

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:27.640
retention, and closure.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:32.430
If the decision can be supported without names, exact locations, full account identifiers,

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:36.320
raw messages, or unrestricted logs, those details should be removed, generalized,

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:36.960
or protected.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.020
More data is not always better. Unnecessary data can increase compliance burden,

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:43.575
create avoidable exposure, and make sharing harder.

00:00:43.575 --> 00:00:48.760
Common privacy controls include redaction, aggregation, pseudonymization, access control,

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:51.880
approved storage, retention limits, and careful product design.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:55.980
Redaction removes details that the audience does not need. Aggregation shows patterns

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:57.820
without exposing individual records.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:02.430
Pseudonymization can support analysis while limiting direct identification. Access control

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.161
limits who can view sensitive records.

00:01:04.161 --> 00:01:08.130
Retention rules prevent intelligence workspaces from becoming permanent stores of data

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:09.515
that should have expired.

00:01:09.515 --> 00:01:13.720
Privacy also affects dissemination. A technical team may need an affected system list.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:17.715
A partner may need a sanitized indicator summary. An executive may need risk and

00:01:17.715 --> 00:01:18.440
recommended action.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.680
A legal reviewer may need source, authority,

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:24.680
and handling context. These audiences do not all need the same raw material.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:28.815
Tailoring the product reduces risk and improves usefulness. Data protection includes

00:01:28.815 --> 00:01:29.920
storage and movement.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.950
Use approved systems, approved sharing channels, markings,

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:36.506
and documented access. Avoid informal channels for sensitive information.

00:01:36.506 --> 00:01:40.360
If cross-border sharing, employee data, customer data, government reporting,

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:43.980
or contractual restrictions are involved, route the issue to qualified reviewers.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:47.950
The safest intelligence product answers the decision question with the least sensitive

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:48.835
detail necessary,

00:01:48.835 --> 00:01:51.510
clear caveats, and a record of how privacy was considered.
